  <bioghist id="c9bcd8360b8af867e7db40b745b1e8b7">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>The Ford Foundation's programming in West Africa started in 1958, and the Foundation opened its first office in the region in 1960 in Lagos, Nigeria. Since that time, the Lagos Office has primarily served as the regional base for the Foundation's program for West Africa. </p><p>During the first two decades of its history, the Lagos Office maintained two different suboffices at different times in the region. The West Africa Program opened an office in Accra, Ghana in 1961. Later, when that office closed, the Foundation opened a suboffice in Abidjan, Cote d'Ivoire (Ivory Coast) in 1974. This office closed in 1978. </p><p>Between 1983 and 1993, the West Africa Program was split between the Lagos Office and an office in Dakar, Senegal (at that time part of the Senegambia confederation), and the Dakar Office was designated as the main office. It was the residence of the Ford Foundation's Representative for West Africa while the Lagos Office was a suboffice. In 1993, the main responsibility for the West Africa Program returned to Lagos, and the Dakar Office later closed in 1996. The Lagos Office has continued to serve as the Ford Foundation's regional Office for West Africa to the present. </p><p>The West Africa Office's programming has historically prioritized activities in Nigeria, Ghana, and Francophone West Africa countries, including Senegal, Cote d'Ivoire, and Mali. These activities have been in the areas of education, agriculture, economic development, family planning, reproductive health, HIV/AIDS, human rights, women's rights, governance, and civil society.</p>  </bioghist>
  <scopecontent id="306f147ef604b330ada043a7f21f4c6c">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>The collection contains the office files of the Ford Foundation's Regional Office for West Africa. Contents reflect how the Foundation's West Africa program has primarily been managed by its office in Lagos, Nigeria. The collection consists of one series of subject files. At this time, collection materials primarily originate from between the 1970s and the early 1980s. </p><p>Collection materials document the West Africa Office's program activities for Nigeria and Francophone West African countries. They show the West Africa Office's program support in the areas of education, agriculture, and family planning. </p><p>Common material formats found throughout the collection include correspondence, memos, reports, grant related documents, meeting materials, drafts, notes, press clips, and publications.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <arrangement id="55ef2ace5901e3c1f1e3c57b62610c64">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>The collection is comprised of one series. Original order was maintained as much as possible.   </p><p>The collection's arrangement is as follows:   </p><p>Series 1. Subject files, 1970-1982</p>  </arrangement>
